          {
            "id": "thm:fixed-transfer-recurrence",
            "proof_tex": "Assume that \\(\\text{oeq:complete-two-pass-facet-stream}\\) has a positive solution.  Write \\(\\rho_L:\\mathcal H_{L+1}\\to\\mathcal H_L\\) for its geometric predecessor map.  The stipulated inverse type-labelled successor table has a fixed nonnegative integer multiplicity\n\\[\nm_{st}=\\#\\{\\text{successor labels producing type }s\n                 \\text{ from one predecessor of type }t\\},\n\\qquad s,t\\in\\mathsf T.\n\\]\nThe table is finite and independent of \\(L\\), so each \\(m_{st}\\) is finite and independent of \\(L\\).  Define the explicit transfer matrix by\n\\[\n(T_\\star)_{st}=m_{st},\\qquad s,t\\in\\mathsf T. \\tag{2}\n\\]\nThis exposes every variable in the claimed recurrence; no matrix is fitted to finitely many values of \\(F_L\\).\n\nFix \\(L\\ge2\\) and \\(s\\in\\mathsf T\\).  Unique predecessorhood partitions the type-\\(s\\) facets in \\(\\mathcal H_{L+1}\\) into the disjoint fibers of \\(\\rho_L\\).  Inverse successorhood says that the type-\\(s\\) part of the fiber above a predecessor \\(g\\) of type \\(t\\) has cardinality \\(m_{st}\\).  Therefore, using only disjoint finite-set counting and then grouping predecessors by type,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(c_{L+1})_s\n&=\\#\\{h\\in\\mathcal H_{L+1}:\\operatorname{type}(h)=s\\}\\\\\n&=\\sum_{g\\in\\mathcal H_L}\n   \\#\\{h\\in\\mathcal H_{L+1}:\\rho_L(h)=g,\n                  \\operatorname{type}(h)=s\\}\\\\\n&=\\sum_{t\\in\\mathsf T}\n   \\sum_{\\substack{g\\in\\mathcal H_L\\\\\\operatorname{type}(g)=t}}m_{st}\\\\\n&=\\sum_{t\\in\\mathsf T}m_{st}(c_L)_t\n=(T_\\star c_L)_s.\n\\end{aligned}\\tag{3}\n\\]\nSince (3) holds for every \\(s\\), it proves \\(c_{L+1}=T_\\star c_L\\).  The finite type labels partition the finite set \\(\\mathcal H_L\\), whence\n\\[\nF_L=|\\mathcal H_L|\n=\\sum_{t\\in\\mathsf T}\\#\\{h\\in\\mathcal H_L:\\operatorname{type}(h)=t\\}\n=\\mathbf 1^\\top c_L. \\tag{4}\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(c_2\\in\\mathbb N^{|\\mathsf T|}\\) is a finite initial vector.  Finally, \\(\\text{lem:small-facet-catalogue-counts}\\) gives the exact, independently certified values\n\\[\nF_2=7,\\qquad F_3=59,\\qquad F_4=197,\\qquad F_5=721.\n\\]\nEquations (2)--(4) prove every assertion of the theorem under precisely its stated positive-solution premise."
          }
        ],
